'''''Ultimate Spider-Man''''' (titled '''''Ultimate Spider-Man: Web Warriors''''' for the third season and '''''Ultimate Spider-Man vs. the Sinister 6''''' for its final season) is an American [[superhero fiction|superhero]] [[cartoon series|animated television series]] broadcast on the cable network [[Disney XD]], based on the [[Spider-Man]] comics published by [[Marvel Comics]]. The series featured writers such as [[Brian Michael Bendis]] (who also created the comic book series [[Ultimate Spider-Man|of the same name]]), [[Paul Dini]], and [[Man of Action Entertainment|Man of Action]] (a group consisting of [[Steven T. Seagle]], [[Joe Kelly (comics writer)|Joe Kelly]], [[Joe Casey]], and [[Duncan Rouleau]]).

It was first announced in summer 2011,<ref name="AirDate">{{cite web |last1=Morra |first1=Tavia |last2=Strom |first2=Marc |date=July 29, 2011 |title=Drake Bell: Meet the Ultimate Spider-Man |url=http://marvel.com/news/story/16379/drake_bell_meet_the_ultimate_spider-man |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110925222312/http://marvel.com/news/story/16379/drake_bell_meet_the_ultimate_spider-man |archive-date=September 25, 2011 |publisher=[[Marvel Comics]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|author=Goellner, Caleb |url=http://www.comicsalliance.com/2011/04/11/marvel-releases-new-ultimate-spider-man-animated-series-promo/ |title=Marvel Releases 'Ultimate Spider-Man' Animated Series Promo Image |work=[[ComicsAlliance]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110810223424/http://www.comicsalliance.com/2011/04/11/marvel-releases-new-ultimate-spider-man-animated-series-promo |archive-date=2011-08-10 }}</ref> and debuted alongside the second season of ''[[The Avengers: Earth's Mightiest Heroes]]'' as part of the ''[[Disney XD#Current|Marvel Universe]]'' programming block on April 1, 2012. In a break from other series, Spider-Man breaks the [[fourth wall]] and speaks directly to the audience. It also includes fantasy sequences from Peter's mind.<ref>{{cite web |last=Collura |first=Scott |date=October 15, 2011 |title=NYCC: Spidey, the Hulk and Agent Coulson Walk into a Comic-Con... |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2011/10/16/nycc-spidey-the-hulk-and-agent-coulson-walk-into-a-comic-con |access-date=February 3, 2022 |website=[[IGN]]}}</ref>

The series concluded its run on January 7, 2017, with the two-part episode "Graduation Day" with four seasons each consisting of 26 episodes. Reruns continued to air on Disney XD until August 30, 2017.

==Synopsis==
===Season 1 (2012) ===
During the first season, Spider-Man is recruited to work for [[S.H.I.E.L.D.]] alongside [[Nova (Sam Alexander)|Nova]], [[White Tiger (Ava Ayala)|White Tiger]], [[Iron Fist (character)|Iron Fist]], and [[Luke Cage]], with whom he attends [[Midtown High School (comics)|Midtown High School]]. Corrupt industrialist [[Norman Osborn]] targets Spider-Man in hopes of collecting his DNA to create a spider-soldier army. To this end, he uses [[Doctor Octopus]] as his pawn, having him send numerous supervillains after Spider-Man and use a sample of Spider-Man's blood to create the [[Venom (character)|Venom]] symbiote, which bonds with Norman's son [[Harry Osborn]]. In the two-part season finale, Doctor Octopus takes revenge on Norman for his treatment of him and transforms him into the [[Green Goblin]]. In the ensuing battle, the S.H.I.E.L.D. [[Helicarrier]] is destroyed.<ref>Hidek, Jeff (January 9, 2012). [http://tv.blogs.starnewsonline.com/14072/nova-getting-animated-on-new-ultimate-spider-man-animated-series/ "Nova getting animated on new Ultimate Spider-Man series on Disney XD"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120422061244/http://tv.blogs.starnewsonline.com/14072/nova-getting-animated-on-new-ultimate-spider-man-animated-series/ |date=2012-04-22 }}. ''[[Star-News]]''.</ref>

===Season 2 (2013) ===
In the second season, the S.H.I.E.L.D. team moves into Peter's house, as they no longer have a home following the Helicarrier's destruction. In the season premiere, after learning that Spider-Man gained his powers from one of Oscorp's genetically altered spiders, Doctor Octopus creates serums with animal DNA in hopes of re-creating the process. S.H.I.E.L.D. scientist Curt Connors, who lost his right arm due to injuries from the Helicarrier's destruction, uses one of the serums to regrow his arm and becomes the [[Lizard (character)|Lizard]]. Later, Doctor Octopus controls the Lizard so that he can join him, [[Electro (Marvel Comics)|Electro]], [[Rhino (character)|Rhino]], [[Kraven the Hunter]], and [[Beetle (comics)|Beetle]] as a member of the [[Sinister Six]]. While the S.H.I.E.L.D. Team defeat five of the six villains, Connors escapes and Spider-Man swears to find and cure him. Later the Sinister Six reappears with Beetle replaced by [[Scorpion (Marvel Comics)|Scorpion]]. During the season, Spider-Man learns more about his teammates' origins: White Tiger became White Tiger after her father [[White Tiger (Hector Ayala)|Hector]] was killed by Kraven, Nova is an orphan who was raised by the [[Guardians of the Galaxy (2008 team)|Guardians of the Galaxy]], Danny Rand became Iron Fist after training in [[K'un-L'un]] and left it to further his training, and Luke Cage received his powers from a version of the Super-Soldier Formula developed by his parents.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20121228042157/http://marvel.com/news/story/19872/ultimate_spider-man_season_2_premieres_jan_21?nav=1 "Ultimate Spider-Man Season 2 Premieres Jan. 21"]. Marvel Comics. December 26, 2012.</ref><ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20130218003515/http://marvel.com/news/story/20135/the_sinister_six_unite_in_ultimate_spider-man "The Sinister Six Unite in Ultimate Spider-Man"]. Marvel Comics. February 14, 2013.</ref> Norman Osborn is cured and becomes [[Iron Patriot]], but Doctor Octopus later transforms him back into the Green Goblin.

===Season 3: ''Web-Warriors'' (2014–2015)===
In the third season, Spider-Man joins the [[Avengers (comics)|Avengers]], but eventually leaves to rejoin S.H.I.E.L.D. In the aftermath, Venom bonds with [[Flash Thompson]] to become Agent Venom and [[Nick Fury]] tasks Spider-Man to recruit other young heroes to form the [[New Warriors]], consisting of [[Ka-Zar (Kevin Plunder)|Ka-Zar]], [[Zabu]], [[Amadeus Cho]] / [[Iron Spider]], and [[Cloak and Dagger (characters)|Cloak and Dagger]]. The New Warriors battle the [[Thunderbolts (comics)|Thunderbolts]], during which Green Goblin escapes with the [[Siege Perilous (comics)|Siege Perilous]] and travels the [[Multiverse (Marvel Comics)|multiverse]] to collect DNA from Spider-Man's counterparts. Chasing after him, Spider-Man meets up with [[Spider-Man 2099]], [[Mayday Parker|Spider-Girl]], [[Spider-Man Noir]], [[Spider-Ham]], Spyder-Knight, and [[Miles Morales]], and works with them to take down Spider-Goblin and [[Electro (Marvel Comics)|Electro]]. After both are defeated and the alternate Spider-Men return to their homes,  the Green Goblin reverts into Norman Osborn and sustains amnesia, making him forget his time as the Green Goblin.<ref>{{cite web| url= https://www.usatoday.com/story/life/tv/2014/06/16/ultimate-spider-man-web-warriors-animated-exclusive/10626739/| title= New 'Ultimate Spider-Man' season expands universe| last= Truitt| first= Brian| work= [[USA Today]] |date= 16 June 2014|access-date=June 16, 2014}}</ref> Afterwards, Spider-Man and his team begin their education at the S.H.I.E.L.D. Academy while dealing with [[Arnim Zola]]. Not long afterwards, [[Aunt May]], Agent Venom, and Iron Spider learn Spider-Man's identity.

===Season 4: ''vs. the Sinister 6'' (2016–2017)===
In the fourth season, Doctor Octopus allies with Zola and [[Hydra (comics)|Hydra]] and forms a new iteration of the Sinister Six consisting of Kraven the Hunter, [[Green Goblin (Ultimate Marvel character)|Ultimate Green Goblin]], Rhino, [[Hydro-Man]], and Electro. In response, Spider-Man forms a team consisting of Agent Venom, Iron Spider, Miles Morales, and [[Scarlet Spider]] ([[Ben Reilly]]). It is later revealed that Ben is a clone of Spider-Man created by Doctor Octopus, but Spider-Man convinces him to reform.<ref name="tvi" />

Later, [[Crossbones (character)|Crossbones]] and [[Morbius]] experiment with Venom, turning New York's citizens into [[Carnage (character)|Carnage]]-like monsters. Carnage possesses [[Mary Jane Watson]], but Spider-Man and Harry Osborn free her. Unbeknownst to Spider-Man, a fragment of the Carnage symbiote remains inside Mary Jane's body.

[[Madame Web]] learns that the destruction of the Siege Perilous shattered the barrier between universes. Spider-Man and Miles Morales travel to alternate realities to collect the fragments of the Siege Perilous and battle [[Wolf Spider (comics)|Wolf Spider]], who seeks to reassemble the Siege Perilous to conquer the multiverse. After reassembling the Siege Perilous, Miles moves to the main universe and lets his version of [[Spider-Woman (Gwen Stacy)|Gwen Stacy]] replace him.

Spider-Man and Mary Jane later discover a second clone of Spider-Man named [[Kaine Parker|Kaine]], who tries to feed off them. During this time, Mary Jane learns to control Carnage and becomes [[Spider-Woman]]. It is discovered that Doctor Octopus and Arnim Zola created Scarlet Spider as part of Weapon S, a project to create synthezoid [[Spider-Slayer]]s. Kaine fuses with the synthezoids to become the Ultimate Spider-Slayer, but is defeated by Agent Venom.

In the series finale, the Sinister Six capture Spider-Man and use an antidote to remove his powers. After working with Norman Osborn, Peter regains his powers and defeats the Sinister Six. Spider-Man convinces Doctor Octopus to surrender, after which he and the S.H.I.E.L.D. trainees graduate.

