==Production==
The series is adapted from the ''[[Ultimate Spider-Man]]'' comic book, which was created by writer [[Brian Michael Bendis]]. Bendis and [[Paul Dini]] served as writers and producers on the show.<ref>{{cite web|author=Johnston, Rich |url=http://www.bleedingcool.com/2010/07/24/brian-bendis-to-produce-ultimate-spider-man-cartoon/ |title=Brian Bendis To Produce And Write For Ultimate Spider-Man Cartoon|publisher=[[Bleeding Cool]]|date=July 24, 2010}}</ref><ref name=Dini>{{cite web|author=Marshall, Rick |url=http://splashpage.mtv.com/2010/08/04/paul-dini-ultimate-spider-man-animated-series/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100805100957/http://splashpage.mtv.com/2010/08/04/paul-dini-ultimate-spider-man-animated-series/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=August 5, 2010 |title=EXCLUSIVE: Paul Dini Joins 'Ultimate Spider-Man' Animated Series, Promises A 'Redefined' Peter Parker |publisher=[[MTV]] |date=2010-08-04 |access-date=2010-08-04}}</ref> Man of Action (a group consisting of [[Steven T. Seagle]], [[Joe Kelly (comics writer)|Joe Kelly]], [[Joe Casey]] and [[Duncan Rouleau]]), the creators of the animated series ''[[Ben 10]]'' and ''[[Generator Rex]]'', serve as supervising producers on the show.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20120920174233/http://www.comicbookresources.com/?page=article&id=40247 "CBR TV: Man of Action's Casey, Rouleau on 'Ultimate Spider-Man,' New Image Projects"]. [[Comic Book Resources]].</ref> Twenty-six episodes were ordered for the first season. According to Paul Dini, the series features a "redefined" Peter Parker, and a combination of frequent guest stars loosely based on Bendis' comics, and original material, such as the origins of some heroes and villains.<ref name=Dini/> Actor [[J. K. Simmons]] reprises his role as [[J. Jonah Jameson]] from [[Sam Raimi]]'s live-action [[Spider-Man (2002 film series)|''Spider-Man'' film trilogy]] for the series.<ref name=Simmons/> Notable voice actors include [[Adrian Pasdar]] as [[Iron Man]] (returning to the role after previously having voiced him in [[Madhouse (studio)|Madhouse]]'s and Marvel's [[Marvel Anime#Iron Man|''Iron Man'' anime]]),<ref name=LATimes>{{cite news|url=http://herocomplex.latimes.com/2011/07/08/iron-man-heroes-flying-man-returns-as-adrian-pasdar-takes-on-anime/|author=Phillips, Jevon|date=July 8, 2011|title='Iron Man': 'Heroes' flying man Adrian Pasdar takes on anime|newspaper=[[Los Angeles Times]]}}</ref><ref name=IGNIronMan>Goldman, Eric (April 20, 2012). [http://tv.ign.com/articles/122/1223457p1.html "Ultimate Spider-Man: Make Way for Iron Man!"]. [[IGN]].</ref> and [[Kevin Michael Richardson]], who voices [[Robbie Robertson (character)|Robbie Robertson]] and [[Bulldozer (character)|Bulldozer]].<ref name=MTVKevinRichardson>{{cite web|url=http://geek-news.mtv.com/2011/08/11/panthro-returns-kevin-michael-richardson-talks-thundercats-green-lantern-and-more/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120522102023/http://geek-news.mtv.com/2011/08/11/panthro-returns-kevin-michael-richardson-talks-thundercats-green-lantern-and-more/|url-status=dead|archive-date=May 22, 2012|title=Kevin Michael Richardson Talks Voicing Panthro And Other Animated Series!|date=August 11, 2011|author=Webb, Charles|publisher=MTV Geek}}</ref>

The series depicts Spider-Man becoming the newest member of S.H.I.E.L.D. under the leadership of Nick Fury, on a team with four other teenage superheroes. Villains such as [[Living Laser]], [[Venom (character)|Venom]], and [[Doctor Doom]] were seen in a trailer shown at the 2011 [[San Diego Comic-Con]].<ref>Mozzocco, J. Caleb (January 10, 2012). [http://www.comicsalliance.com/2012/01/10/ultimate-spider-man-cartoon-disney-xd-marvel-programming-block-april-1/ "'Ultimate Spider-Man' Cartoon to Kick Off Disney XD Marvel Programming Block April 1"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120511053105/http://www.comicsalliance.com/2012/01/10/ultimate-spider-man-cartoon-disney-xd-marvel-programming-block-april-1/ |date=2012-05-11 }}. ComicsAlliance.</ref>

''Ultimate Spider-Man'' premiered on April 1, 2012, on Disney XD in the United States, while the pilot episode was released on [[Xbox Live]] and [[PlayStation Store]] on April 2, 2012.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.usatoday.com/life/comics/story/2012-03-07/Ultimate-Spider-Man-Marvel-Universe-animated-series/53396068/1|title='Ultimate Spider-Man' leads Disney XD's new superhero series|access-date=March 19, 2012|date=March 7, 2012|newspaper=[[USA Today]]|last=Truitt|first=Brian}}</ref> In the UK and Ireland, the show premiered on [[Disney XD (UK and Ireland)|Disney UK and Ireland]] on May 31, 2013. It premiered in Canada on June 22, 2012, on [[Teletoon (Canada)|Teletoon]].

Disney XD and Marvel officially announced Season 3 on July 20, 2013, at San Diego Comic-Con.<ref name=Marvel.com>[https://web.archive.org/web/20130722131742/http://marvel.com/news/story/20911/sdcc_2013_ultimate_spider-man_returns_for_season_3 "SDCC 2013: Ultimate Spider-Man Returns For Season 3"]. ''Marvel.com''. July 20, 2013.</ref> Season 3, retitled ''Ultimate Spider-Man: Web-Warriors'', includes Spider-Man joining the [[Avengers (comics)|Avengers]] (consisting of the line-up from ''[[Avengers Assemble (TV series)|Avengers Assemble]]'') and introducing characters such as [[Cloak and Dagger (characters)|Cloak and Dagger]], [[Amadeus Cho]], [[Ka-Zar (Kevin Plunder)|Ka-Zar]], and [[Agent Venom]].<ref name=Marvel.com/>

A fourth season, ''Ultimate Spider-Man vs. the Sinister 6'', began broadcasting on February 21, 2016.<ref name=tvi>{{cite news|last1=Sands|first1=Rich|title=Disney XD Renews (and Retitles) Marvel's Avengers and Ultimate Spider-Man Animated Series|url=https://www.tvinsider.com/article/1986/disney-xd-renews-and-renames-marvel-animated-series-avengers-ultimate-spider-man/|access-date=June 2, 2015|work=TV Insider|date=June 1, 2015}}</ref>

